window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2022-06-2", "source": "2022年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part,  you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence “Nowadays more and more people keep learning new skills to adapt to a fast-changing world.” You can make comments, cite examples, or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Nowadays more and more people keep learning new skills to adapt to a fast-changing world. This trend, in my\nview, is not a passing fashion but a wise and even necessary response to the age we live in.\nThe most obvious reason lies in the speed of technological change. Skills that guaranteed a stable job ten years\nago may become outdated overnight, so those who stop learning risk being replaced. Take my cousin as an\nexample. As a traditional accountant, she felt threatened when her company introduced financial software, yet\ninstead of complaining she spent her evenings mastering data analysis. Within a year she was promoted rather\nthan laid off. Her experience convinces me that continuous learning is what keeps a person competitive.\nBeyond the workplace, learning also enriches our daily life and broadens our horizons.\nIn conclusion, keeping pace with a changing world through lifelong learning is no longer optional but essential.\nRather than fearing change, we should treat every new skill as a bridge to a wider future. I firmly believe that\nwhoever embraces new skills today will stand firm tomorrow."}, "translation": {"prompt": "南京长江大桥是长江上首座由中国设计、采用国产材料建造的铁路、公路两用桥，上层的4车道公路桥长4589米，下层的双轨道铁路桥长6772米。铁路桥连接原来的天津一浦口和上海―南京两条铁路线，使火车过江从过去一个半小时缩短为现在的2分钟。大桥是南北交通的重要枢纽，也是南京的著名景点之一。 南京长江大桥的建成标志着中国桥梁建设的一个飞跃，大大方便了长江两岸的物资交流和人员来往，对促进经济发展和改善人民生活起到了巨大作用。", "sample": "The Nanjing Yangtze River Bridge is the first road-rail bridge over the Yangtze River that was designed by China and built with domestic materials. Its upper deck, a four-lane highway, is 4,589 meters long, while its lower deck, a double-track railway, is 6,772 meters long. The railway bridge connects the two former railway lines, the Tianjin-Pukou line and the Shanghai-Nanjing line, reducing the time for trains to cross the river from one and a half hours in the past to just two minutes now. The bridge is both an important hub for north-south transportation and one of the famous tourist attractions in Nanjing. The completion of the Nanjing Yangtze River Bridge marks a great leap in China’s bridge construction. It has greatly facilitated the exchange of goods and the movement of people between the two banks of the Yangtze River, and has played a huge role in promoting economic development and improving people’s lives."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Thinking kind thoughts about yourself and your loved ones can prove beneficial for your overall wellbeing, empirical evidence has shown. Researchers carried out an investigation to explore the  __26__  between having kind thoughts and a person's psychological state.\nFor the study, five groups of participants were presented with a different set of audio instructions, some of which encouraged the participants to think  __27__  about themselves and others which persuaded them to think in a self-critical manner. After listening to the audio instructions, the participants were asked to answer a series of questions. These included whether they felt agitated, how likely they were to show kindness to themselves and how  __28__  they felt to other individuals.\nThe participants who were instructed to think kindly about themselves were more likely to exhibit a bodily response associated with being relaxed and feeling safe. Their heart rates also dropped, which is a healthy sign of a heart that can respond flexibly to situations. Yet,  __29__  , those who listened to the critical audio clips were noted as having a higher heart rate and sweat response afterwards, both of which  __30__  feelings of threat and distress.\nHaving the ability to switch off the body's natural threat response can  __31__  a person's immune system. This, in turn, gives them a greater likelihood of recovering quickly from illness. These findings help us to further understand some of our clinical trials research findings, where we show that individuals with  __32__  depression benefit particularly from self-awareness-based  __33__  therapy. They essentially learn to become more sympathetic to themselves.\nThe sense is that for people  __34__  to depression, meeting their negative thoughts and feelings with  __35__  is a radically different way; that these thoughts are not facts. It introduces a different way of being and knowing that is quite transformative for many people.", "matchIntro": "Saving Our Planet", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "In the long view, the human relationship with forests has been one of brutal destruction, but even it carries elements of slow hope. In the Middle Ages, there was no shortage of timber in most parts of the world, and few saw cutting down forests as a problem. Yet in 1548 the people of Venice estimated that an important timber supply would last only 30 years at their current rate of usage—but different forest management would make it possible to meet the demand for many centuries to come. The idea of preserving resources came out of a concern for the future: a fear of using up resources faster than they could be replenished(补充)."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Economic interests were at the core of this understanding of trees and forests. It would take more than three centuries before scientists began to understand that timber production is not the only, and possibly not the most important, function of forests. The late 19th and early 20th century saw an increasing recognition that forests serve as habitats for countless animal and plant species that all rely on each other. They take over protective functions against soil erosion and landslides(塌方); they make a significant contribution to the water balance as they prevent surface runoff; they filter dirt particles, greenhouse gases and radioactive substances from the air; they produce oxygen; they provide spaces for recreation and they preserve historic and prehistoric remains. As a result, forests around the world have been set aside as parks or wilderness areas."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Recent years have seen a big change in our view of forests. Peter Wohlleben's book The Hidden Life of Trees (2015), an international bestseller, suggests that trees can warn each other of danger through a \"wood wide web\" of roots and fungi(真菌). They support each other through sharing of nutrients and information, and they even keep ancient stumps alive by feeding them solutions of sugars. Such insights have made us aware of deep ecological relationships between humans and the more-than-human world."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Awareness of ecologies is a recent phenomenon. It was not until the 1940s that the concept of the \"environment\" embracing all living and nonliving things developed. In the 1970s, the term \"environment\" gained currency, becoming widely adopted in the English and Romance languages, and as \"Umwelt\" (\"surrounding world\") in German. The emergence of the idea led to the rise of environmental agencies, regulations and environmental studies, and to environmental science as new, integrated academic disciplines. It was in 1956 that the very first bachelor of science in environmental studies was awarded, at the State University of New York College of Forestry at Syracuse. Since the 1970s—with the rise of \"environmentalism\"—environmental studies programmes have sprung up at hundreds of universities. There is (slow) hope in the fact that scholars from many different disciplines have adopted the term \"environment\" over the past decades. They are exploring intricate connections within and between complex ecologies, as well as the impact that human environment-making (through techno-industrial, economic and other manipulative developments) has had on the biosphere."}, {"label": "E", "text": "The rise of the idea of the environment and a scholarly understanding of ecological processes has influenced new technologies and also politics. We have come to ask questions about vulnerability and risk, world ecologies, and the relationship between nature and power. The search for an adequate response to climate change occupies centre stage in international diplomacy."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Social and environmental activists, scientists and indigenous groups have called the Paris Agreement of the United Nations Climate Change Conference in December 2015 insufficient, weak, or compromised. To some extent, they are right: climate change has already destroyed tens of thousands of livelihoods, and the situation will worsen in the near future for millions of mostly poorer people, who will join the ranks of those who have already been displaced by climate change and extreme weather events. But the Paris Conference nevertheless marked a historic step toward the recognition of the need for action on climate change, the cutting of carbon emissions, and world cooperation. There were 195 nations that came to the table in Paris and agreed to limits on emissions. Historically, nothing comparable had happened prior to this. Before the 20th century, a handful of scientists had been interested in the theoretical relationship between greenhouse gases and climate change, but only the empirical evidence accumulated since the late 20th century established a clear connection between the burning of fossil fuels and a vastly accelerated rise in global temperatures."}, {"label": "G", "text": "The current crisis is not the first that humans have encountered, and a look at the struggles with pollution in recent history reveals transformations that once seemed unimaginable. The \"London fog\" that came to define the capital through British novels and thrillers is in reality smog or smoke, a legacy of industrialisation. After a century of ignorance, London was hit by the Great Smog of December 1952—the worst air-pollution event in the history of the United Kingdom which caused the deaths of approximately 12,000 people. Shortly thereafter, public initiatives and political campaigns led to strict regulations and new laws, including the Clean Air Act (1956). Today, London has effectively reduced traffic emissions through the introduction of a Congestion Charge Zone in 2003, and an Ultra Low Emission Zone in 2019."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Scientific evidence that we are living in an era of climate change, resource exhaustion and potential ecological disaster is overwhelming. How do we motivate a public exhausted by never-ending scenarios of doom and disaster, when the challenges seem so huge and so impossible to solve? Statistics about extinction and the gloom of decline will not in themselves get us out of our often self-created ecological traps: instead, they are more likely to result in paralysis and inaction."}, {"label": "I", "text": "We need stories and histories of change and transformation: ecological stories that make us confront the fact that human power is potentially destructive, and that the survival of our species on this planet depends on the preservation of soil and water, and the habitats and ecological systems."}, {"label": "J", "text": "It is time that we showed successes and accelerations in ecological awareness, action and restoration: stories that include past successes and future visions about the rise of urban gardening and of renaturalised riverscapes, of successful protests against polluted air and water, of the rise of regional markets and slow food, and the planting of trees around the globe, of initiatives and enterprises that work towards ecological restoration. The reality of ecological curses seems far greater than the power of the hopes left at the bottom of Pandora's box. But if we believe that nothing can be changed, then we are giving up our opportunity to act."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Today's saving powers will not come from a deus ex machina(解围之神). In an ever-more complex and synthetic world, our saving powers won't come from a single source, and certainly not from a too-big-to-fail approach or from those who have been drawn into the whirlpool of our age of speed. Hope can work as a wakeup call. It acknowledges setbacks. The concept of slow hope suggests that we can't expect things to change overnight. If the ever-faster exhaustion of natural resources (in ecological terms) and the \"shrinking of the present\" (in social terms) are urgent problems of humans, then cutting down on exhaustive practices and working towards a \"stretching of the present\" will be ways to move forward."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Since American idol star Taryn Southern started composing music with AI in 2017, musicians all over the world have begun wondering about the implications of AI and modern technology where music production is concerned. Using AI in the creation of music is perceived by some as a helpful tool and by others as almost \"the beginning of the end\".\nIn Taryn's case, AI software enabled her to communicate melodies and chords that she didn't know how to put together herself. The end product was therefore a collaborative effort, rather than a piece entirely produced by technology. Taryn's story has a distinctly positive feel that highlights the advantages of using AI in music production. It can serve as a source of inspiration, and as an ideal jumping-off point should a musician be hit with writer's block(文思枯竭).\nContrary to seeing AI as a tool, some musicians consider it to be hugely detrimental to the music scene. At the moment, because such technology is still so young, the music it's producing is not necessarily what we want to hear. In short, it's not of great quality. Those who have produced their own music, or even fans of authentic, artistic music, will also argue that a computer could never emulate the work (and human touch) of a true musician.\nMusic has been an integral part of the story of humans for ages; in fact, the first known piece of music is believed to be around 3,400 years old. Songs have long been used as a means of communicating messages and folk stories, covering everything from societal ethics to world history. Since many people see music as such an inherently human expression, it is often considered as too precious to impart to technology. The thought of a computer generating a \"random\" piece of music that hasn't been painstakingly created by an artist is almost seen as sacrilegious(亵渎神圣的).\nRegardless of which side of the argument you fall on, it seems likely that the use of AI in music production will only become more frequent. Our modern world is preoccupied with technological advancements. Instead of shying away from the idea of this bleak future, the best approach to take is one of optimism and curiosity. While there are always bound to be stubborn old-school musicians who refuse to use tech, music producers should consider AI as something to be embraced. AI music software is still very much in its infancy, but with more investors interested in the development and outcomes of such technology, and considering the rapid growth rate of other tech advances in recent years, it's only a matter of time before AI-produced music is seen as the new norm.", "47": "Since American idol star Taryn Southern started composing music with AI in 2017, musicians all over the world have begun wondering about the implications of AI and modern technology where music production is concerned. Using AI in the creation of music is perceived by some as a helpful tool and by others as almost \"the beginning of the end\".\nIn Taryn's case, AI software enabled her to communicate melodies and chords that she didn't know how to put together herself. The end product was therefore a collaborative effort, rather than a piece entirely produced by technology. Taryn's story has a distinctly positive feel that highlights the advantages of using AI in music production. It can serve as a source of inspiration, and as an ideal jumping-off point should a musician be hit with writer's block(文思枯竭).\nContrary to seeing AI as a tool, some musicians consider it to be hugely detrimental to the music scene. At the moment, because such technology is still so young, the music it's producing is not necessarily what we want to hear. In short, it's not of great quality. Those who have produced their own music, or even fans of authentic, artistic music, will also argue that a computer could never emulate the work (and human touch) of a true musician.\nMusic has been an integral part of the story of humans for ages; in fact, the first known piece of music is believed to be around 3,400 years old. Songs have long been used as a means of communicating messages and folk stories, covering everything from societal ethics to world history. Since many people see music as such an inherently human expression, it is often considered as too precious to impart to technology. The thought of a computer generating a \"random\" piece of music that hasn't been painstakingly created by an artist is almost seen as sacrilegious(亵渎神圣的).\nRegardless of which side of the argument you fall on, it seems likely that the use of AI in music production will only become more frequent. Our modern world is preoccupied with technological advancements. Instead of shying away from the idea of this bleak future, the best approach to take is one of optimism and curiosity. While there are always bound to be stubborn old-school musicians who refuse to use tech, music producers should consider AI as something to be embraced. AI music software is still very much in its infancy, but with more investors interested in the development and outcomes of such technology, and considering the rapid growth rate of other tech advances in recent years, it's only a matter of time before AI-produced music is seen as the new norm.", "48": "Since American idol star Taryn Southern started composing music with AI in 2017, musicians all over the world have begun wondering about the implications of AI and modern technology where music production is concerned. Using AI in the creation of music is perceived by some as a helpful tool and by others as almost \"the beginning of the end\".\nIn Taryn's case, AI software enabled her to communicate melodies and chords that she didn't know how to put together herself. The end product was therefore a collaborative effort, rather than a piece entirely produced by technology. Taryn's story has a distinctly positive feel that highlights the advantages of using AI in music production. It can serve as a source of inspiration, and as an ideal jumping-off point should a musician be hit with writer's block(文思枯竭).\nContrary to seeing AI as a tool, some musicians consider it to be hugely detrimental to the music scene. At the moment, because such technology is still so young, the music it's producing is not necessarily what we want to hear. In short, it's not of great quality. Those who have produced their own music, or even fans of authentic, artistic music, will also argue that a computer could never emulate the work (and human touch) of a true musician.\nMusic has been an integral part of the story of humans for ages; in fact, the first known piece of music is believed to be around 3,400 years old. Songs have long been used as a means of communicating messages and folk stories, covering everything from societal ethics to world history. Since many people see music as such an inherently human expression, it is often considered as too precious to impart to technology. The thought of a computer generating a \"random\" piece of music that hasn't been painstakingly created by an artist is almost seen as sacrilegious(亵渎神圣的).\nRegardless of which side of the argument you fall on, it seems likely that the use of AI in music production will only become more frequent. Our modern world is preoccupied with technological advancements. Instead of shying away from the idea of this bleak future, the best approach to take is one of optimism and curiosity. While there are always bound to be stubborn old-school musicians who refuse to use tech, music producers should consider AI as something to be embraced. AI music software is still very much in its infancy, but with more investors interested in the development and outcomes of such technology, and considering the rapid growth rate of other tech advances in recent years, it's only a matter of time before AI-produced music is seen as the new norm.", "49": "Since American idol star Taryn Southern started composing music with AI in 2017, musicians all over the world have begun wondering about the implications of AI and modern technology where music production is concerned. Using AI in the creation of music is perceived by some as a helpful tool and by others as almost \"the beginning of the end\".\nIn Taryn's case, AI software enabled her to communicate melodies and chords that she didn't know how to put together herself. The end product was therefore a collaborative effort, rather than a piece entirely produced by technology. Taryn's story has a distinctly positive feel that highlights the advantages of using AI in music production. It can serve as a source of inspiration, and as an ideal jumping-off point should a musician be hit with writer's block(文思枯竭).\nContrary to seeing AI as a tool, some musicians consider it to be hugely detrimental to the music scene. At the moment, because such technology is still so young, the music it's producing is not necessarily what we want to hear. In short, it's not of great quality. Those who have produced their own music, or even fans of authentic, artistic music, will also argue that a computer could never emulate the work (and human touch) of a true musician.\nMusic has been an integral part of the story of humans for ages; in fact, the first known piece of music is believed to be around 3,400 years old. Songs have long been used as a means of communicating messages and folk stories, covering everything from societal ethics to world history. Since many people see music as such an inherently human expression, it is often considered as too precious to impart to technology. The thought of a computer generating a \"random\" piece of music that hasn't been painstakingly created by an artist is almost seen as sacrilegious(亵渎神圣的).\nRegardless of which side of the argument you fall on, it seems likely that the use of AI in music production will only become more frequent. Our modern world is preoccupied with technological advancements. Instead of shying away from the idea of this bleak future, the best approach to take is one of optimism and curiosity. While there are always bound to be stubborn old-school musicians who refuse to use tech, music producers should consider AI as something to be embraced. AI music software is still very much in its infancy, but with more investors interested in the development and outcomes of such technology, and considering the rapid growth rate of other tech advances in recent years, it's only a matter of time before AI-produced music is seen as the new norm.", "50": "Since American idol star Taryn Southern started composing music with AI in 2017, musicians all over the world have begun wondering about the implications of AI and modern technology where music production is concerned. Using AI in the creation of music is perceived by some as a helpful tool and by others as almost \"the beginning of the end\".\nIn Taryn's case, AI software enabled her to communicate melodies and chords that she didn't know how to put together herself. The end product was therefore a collaborative effort, rather than a piece entirely produced by technology. Taryn's story has a distinctly positive feel that highlights the advantages of using AI in music production. It can serve as a source of inspiration, and as an ideal jumping-off point should a musician be hit with writer's block(文思枯竭).\nContrary to seeing AI as a tool, some musicians consider it to be hugely detrimental to the music scene. At the moment, because such technology is still so young, the music it's producing is not necessarily what we want to hear. In short, it's not of great quality. Those who have produced their own music, or even fans of authentic, artistic music, will also argue that a computer could never emulate the work (and human touch) of a true musician.\nMusic has been an integral part of the story of humans for ages; in fact, the first known piece of music is believed to be around 3,400 years old. Songs have long been used as a means of communicating messages and folk stories, covering everything from societal ethics to world history. Since many people see music as such an inherently human expression, it is often considered as too precious to impart to technology. The thought of a computer generating a \"random\" piece of music that hasn't been painstakingly created by an artist is almost seen as sacrilegious(亵渎神圣的).\nRegardless of which side of the argument you fall on, it seems likely that the use of AI in music production will only become more frequent. Our modern world is preoccupied with technological advancements. Instead of shying away from the idea of this bleak future, the best approach to take is one of optimism and curiosity. While there are always bound to be stubborn old-school musicians who refuse to use tech, music producers should consider AI as something to be embraced. AI music software is still very much in its infancy, but with more investors interested in the development and outcomes of such technology, and considering the rapid growth rate of other tech advances in recent years, it's only a matter of time before AI-produced music is seen as the new norm.", "51": "A few weeks ago, a well-meaning professor tried to explain the physiological process behind viruses and the human body in a tweet and was immediately criticized for a mistake in his information. He then issued an apology and deleted his erroneous tweet.\nCommunicating science beyond the academic bubble is necessary to augmenting public understanding of health and environmental issues and helping individuals make well-informed personal decisions.\nHowever, scientists who engage in science communication must acknowledge that even in their area, their expertise is deep but narrow. They need to recognize the constraints in their own knowledge. That is not to suggest that they only write or present on their own research, but rather, that they consult with an expert if the topic is outside of their discipline. Fact-checking with a scientist who works in the specialty will prevent the unintentional spread of misinformation, and the process of doing so may yield tiny pieces of interesting new information that can be incorporated.\nSome have argued that the public is not educated enough to understand scientific information, especially for any complex phenomena, but this is absurd. Science instruction can be found at all levels of public education with most secondary schools offering classes on biology, physics, and chemistry. If anything, social media has shown that the public craves knowledge based on a solid scientific foundation. Even the public discourse that follows most scientific articles shows that online readers can understand even the most baffling of scientific principles.\nIt is equally imperative to emphasize that being an expert on a topic does not automatically make a scholar qualified to communicate it to a nonscientific audience. A number of scientists recently have been offering public-aimed explanations of scientific phenomena. Even though they have appropriate credentials, they often do very little in the way of explaining. One biologist shared an intricate analogy involving a library, books, paper, a recipe, ingredients, and a cake to explain the process behind vaccines. Any explanation that requires a written key to keep track of what each item represents is not a clear example for public consumption.\nScience communication is a science in and of itself. It requires rigorous training and instruction. A scientist should take communication courses that can teach a person how to identify and eliminate jargon and how to develop effective analogies to explain complex concepts. One cannot assume communication expertise—imagine if someone just decided that they were a physicist and started trying to contribute to the field without the necessary background. Doing a poor job communicating science to the public will only create confusion and widen the gap between science and society, a gap that scientists are trying to close.", "52": "A few weeks ago, a well-meaning professor tried to explain the physiological process behind viruses and the human body in a tweet and was immediately criticized for a mistake in his information. He then issued an apology and deleted his erroneous tweet.\nCommunicating science beyond the academic bubble is necessary to augmenting public understanding of health and environmental issues and helping individuals make well-informed personal decisions.\nHowever, scientists who engage in science communication must acknowledge that even in their area, their expertise is deep but narrow. They need to recognize the constraints in their own knowledge. That is not to suggest that they only write or present on their own research, but rather, that they consult with an expert if the topic is outside of their discipline. Fact-checking with a scientist who works in the specialty will prevent the unintentional spread of misinformation, and the process of doing so may yield tiny pieces of interesting new information that can be incorporated.\nSome have argued that the public is not educated enough to understand scientific information, especially for any complex phenomena, but this is absurd. Science instruction can be found at all levels of public education with most secondary schools offering classes on biology, physics, and chemistry. If anything, social media has shown that the public craves knowledge based on a solid scientific foundation. Even the public discourse that follows most scientific articles shows that online readers can understand even the most baffling of scientific principles.\nIt is equally imperative to emphasize that being an expert on a topic does not automatically make a scholar qualified to communicate it to a nonscientific audience. A number of scientists recently have been offering public-aimed explanations of scientific phenomena. Even though they have appropriate credentials, they often do very little in the way of explaining. One biologist shared an intricate analogy involving a library, books, paper, a recipe, ingredients, and a cake to explain the process behind vaccines. Any explanation that requires a written key to keep track of what each item represents is not a clear example for public consumption.\nScience communication is a science in and of itself. It requires rigorous training and instruction. A scientist should take communication courses that can teach a person how to identify and eliminate jargon and how to develop effective analogies to explain complex concepts. One cannot assume communication expertise—imagine if someone just decided that they were a physicist and started trying to contribute to the field without the necessary background. Doing a poor job communicating science to the public will only create confusion and widen the gap between science and society, a gap that scientists are trying to close.", "53": "A few weeks ago, a well-meaning professor tried to explain the physiological process behind viruses and the human body in a tweet and was immediately criticized for a mistake in his information. He then issued an apology and deleted his erroneous tweet.\nCommunicating science beyond the academic bubble is necessary to augmenting public understanding of health and environmental issues and helping individuals make well-informed personal decisions.\nHowever, scientists who engage in science communication must acknowledge that even in their area, their expertise is deep but narrow. They need to recognize the constraints in their own knowledge. That is not to suggest that they only write or present on their own research, but rather, that they consult with an expert if the topic is outside of their discipline. Fact-checking with a scientist who works in the specialty will prevent the unintentional spread of misinformation, and the process of doing so may yield tiny pieces of interesting new information that can be incorporated.\nSome have argued that the public is not educated enough to understand scientific information, especially for any complex phenomena, but this is absurd. Science instruction can be found at all levels of public education with most secondary schools offering classes on biology, physics, and chemistry. If anything, social media has shown that the public craves knowledge based on a solid scientific foundation. Even the public discourse that follows most scientific articles shows that online readers can understand even the most baffling of scientific principles.\nIt is equally imperative to emphasize that being an expert on a topic does not automatically make a scholar qualified to communicate it to a nonscientific audience. A number of scientists recently have been offering public-aimed explanations of scientific phenomena. Even though they have appropriate credentials, they often do very little in the way of explaining. One biologist shared an intricate analogy involving a library, books, paper, a recipe, ingredients, and a cake to explain the process behind vaccines. Any explanation that requires a written key to keep track of what each item represents is not a clear example for public consumption.\nScience communication is a science in and of itself. It requires rigorous training and instruction. A scientist should take communication courses that can teach a person how to identify and eliminate jargon and how to develop effective analogies to explain complex concepts. One cannot assume communication expertise—imagine if someone just decided that they were a physicist and started trying to contribute to the field without the necessary background. Doing a poor job communicating science to the public will only create confusion and widen the gap between science and society, a gap that scientists are trying to close.", "54": "A few weeks ago, a well-meaning professor tried to explain the physiological process behind viruses and the human body in a tweet and was immediately criticized for a mistake in his information. He then issued an apology and deleted his erroneous tweet.\nCommunicating science beyond the academic bubble is necessary to augmenting public understanding of health and environmental issues and helping individuals make well-informed personal decisions.\nHowever, scientists who engage in science communication must acknowledge that even in their area, their expertise is deep but narrow. They need to recognize the constraints in their own knowledge. That is not to suggest that they only write or present on their own research, but rather, that they consult with an expert if the topic is outside of their discipline. Fact-checking with a scientist who works in the specialty will prevent the unintentional spread of misinformation, and the process of doing so may yield tiny pieces of interesting new information that can be incorporated.\nSome have argued that the public is not educated enough to understand scientific information, especially for any complex phenomena, but this is absurd. Science instruction can be found at all levels of public education with most secondary schools offering classes on biology, physics, and chemistry. If anything, social media has shown that the public craves knowledge based on a solid scientific foundation. Even the public discourse that follows most scientific articles shows that online readers can understand even the most baffling of scientific principles.\nIt is equally imperative to emphasize that being an expert on a topic does not automatically make a scholar qualified to communicate it to a nonscientific audience. A number of scientists recently have been offering public-aimed explanations of scientific phenomena. Even though they have appropriate credentials, they often do very little in the way of explaining. One biologist shared an intricate analogy involving a library, books, paper, a recipe, ingredients, and a cake to explain the process behind vaccines. Any explanation that requires a written key to keep track of what each item represents is not a clear example for public consumption.\nScience communication is a science in and of itself. It requires rigorous training and instruction. A scientist should take communication courses that can teach a person how to identify and eliminate jargon and how to develop effective analogies to explain complex concepts. One cannot assume communication expertise—imagine if someone just decided that they were a physicist and started trying to contribute to the field without the necessary background. Doing a poor job communicating science to the public will only create confusion and widen the gap between science and society, a gap that scientists are trying to close.", "55": "A few weeks ago, a well-meaning professor tried to explain the physiological process behind viruses and the human body in a tweet and was immediately criticized for a mistake in his information. He then issued an apology and deleted his erroneous tweet.\nCommunicating science beyond the academic bubble is necessary to augmenting public understanding of health and environmental issues and helping individuals make well-informed personal decisions.\nHowever, scientists who engage in science communication must acknowledge that even in their area, their expertise is deep but narrow. They need to recognize the constraints in their own knowledge. That is not to suggest that they only write or present on their own research, but rather, that they consult with an expert if the topic is outside of their discipline. Fact-checking with a scientist who works in the specialty will prevent the unintentional spread of misinformation, and the process of doing so may yield tiny pieces of interesting new information that can be incorporated.\nSome have argued that the public is not educated enough to understand scientific information, especially for any complex phenomena, but this is absurd. Science instruction can be found at all levels of public education with most secondary schools offering classes on biology, physics, and chemistry. If anything, social media has shown that the public craves knowledge based on a solid scientific foundation. Even the public discourse that follows most scientific articles shows that online readers can understand even the most baffling of scientific principles.\nIt is equally imperative to emphasize that being an expert on a topic does not automatically make a scholar qualified to communicate it to a nonscientific audience. A number of scientists recently have been offering public-aimed explanations of scientific phenomena. Even though they have appropriate credentials, they often do very little in the way of explaining. One biologist shared an intricate analogy involving a library, books, paper, a recipe, ingredients, and a cake to explain the process behind vaccines. Any explanation that requires a written key to keep track of what each item represents is not a clear example for public consumption.\nScience communication is a science in and of itself. It requires rigorous training and instruction. A scientist should take communication courses that can teach a person how to identify and eliminate jargon and how to develop effective analogies to explain complex concepts. One cannot assume communication expertise—imagine if someone just decided that they were a physicist and started trying to contribute to the field without the necessary background. Doing a poor job communicating science to the public will only create confusion and widen the gap between science and society, a gap that scientists are trying to close."}, "questions": [{"id": "2022-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adversely", "B) amiably", "C) boost", "D) cognitive", "E) compassion", "F) connected", "G) correlation", "H) fascinated", "I) indignantly", "J) insulation", "K) lavish", "L) prone", "M) recurrent", "N) signify", "O) surpass"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) correlation\n名词\n【词性槽】定冠词 the 后 + 介词 between 前，须填名词\n【依据】explore the correlation between A and B 是固定搭配\n题干 explore the __(26)__ between having kind thoughts and a person's\npsychological state 正是研究『两个变量之间的相关性』的标准说法，correlation\nbetween A and B 在学术英语中是高频搭配。\n【竞争词】E) compassion 词义虽贴主题，但与 between A and B 搭不上\ncompassion 表『同情心』，不能说『探索善念与心理状态之间的同情』，逻辑不通；且\ncompassion 在第 35 空有更精准的归宿，留着备用。\nJ) insulation 词义『绝缘/隔离』与研究主题完全无关\ninsulation 多用于物理/工程语境，放在 explore the __(26)__ between 中说『探索…之间的绝\n缘』明显荒谬。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·定冠词 the 后 + 介词 betwe"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adversely", "B) amiably", "C) boost", "D) cognitive", "E) compassion", "F) connected", "G) correlation", "H) fascinated", "I) indignantly", "J) insulation", "K) lavish", "L) prone", "M) recurrent", "N) signify", "O) surpass"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) amiably\n副词\n【词性槽】动词 think 之后修饰动词，须填副词\n【依据】与后文 self-critical 形成正反对照\n本句结构 some of which encouraged the participants to think __(27)__ about\nthemselves and others which persuaded them to think in a self-critical manner\n——前半『鼓励善待自己地想』、后半『劝其自我批判地想』，是一对反义。amiably\n（友好地）正与 self-critical（自我批判）相反，呼应主旨 Thinking kind thoughts\nabout yourself。\n【竞争词】A) adversely 义为『不利地』，方向与 self-critical 同\nadversely 偏负面，无法与后半 self-critical 构成对照；且第 29 空 yet 转折更需要它。\nI) indignantly 义为『愤慨地』，色彩负面且不合主旨\nindignantly 表强烈不满，与『善待自己』完全相反，逻辑不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·动词 think 之后修饰动词，须填副词"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adversely", "B) amiably", "C) boost", "D) cognitive", "E) compassion", "F) connected", "G) correlation", "H) fascinated", "I) indignantly", "J) insulation", "K) lavish", "L) prone", "M) recurrent", "N) signify", "O) surpass"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) connected\n形容词\n【词性槽】系动词 felt 后作表语，且与 how 引导的程度副词搭配\n【依据】feel connected to sb 是高频固定搭配\nhow __(28)__ they felt to other individuals——『他们感到与他人有多紧密的联系』，\nfeel connected to 是描述人际联结感的标准表达，与上句『对自己友善』并列考察人际维\n度，自然落地。\n【竞争词】H) fascinated fascinated 搭 by/with，不搭 to\nfascinated to 不构成固定搭配；语义『对他人着迷』也不符合研究问卷情境。\nK) lavish lavish 不能与 felt to 搭配\nlavish 表『奢华的』，无法与『对他人感到』语义对接。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·系动词 felt 后作表语，且与 how"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adversely", "B) amiably", "C) boost", "D) cognitive", "E) compassion", "F) connected", "G) correlation", "H) fascinated", "I) indignantly", "J) insulation", "K) lavish", "L) prone", "M) recurrent", "N) signify", "O) surpass"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) adversely\n副词\n【词性槽】句首逗号隔开的独立修饰位，副词作状语\n【依据】Yet 后承接转折，需『相反地』对接前段善念组的正面反应\n前段 The participants who were instructed to think kindly about themselves were\nmore likely to exhibit a bodily response associated with being relaxed——善念组放\n松；本句 Yet, __(29)__, those who listened to the critical audio clips were noted as\nhaving a higher heart rate——批判组反而心率升高。adversely 在此 = 『反之、不利\n地』，把转折讲透。\n【竞争词】I) indignantly 义为『愤慨地』，描述情绪而非研究结果走向\nindignantly 只能修饰人发火的方式，不能作整句状语标示『相反走向』；不符合研究客观叙述\n语体。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 I) —— 考生易把 indignantly 当作『批判组』的情绪描述塞进 29\n空。Yet, __(29)__, those who listened to the critical audio clips 看到『批判录音』易联\n想『愤慨』；防坑：本空在 Yet 后逗号夹的位置，要的是表示『反向走向』的副词\n（adversely），不是描述参与者情绪的副词。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·句首逗号隔开的独立修饰位，副词作状语"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adversely", "B) amiably", "C) boost", "D) cognitive", "E) compassion", "F) connected", "G) correlation", "H) fascinated", "I) indignantly", "J) insulation", "K) lavish", "L) prone", "M) recurrent", "N) signify", "O) surpass"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) signify\n动词\n【词性槽】定语从句主语 both of which 后须填谓语动词\n【依据】signify feelings of threat 表『标示/意味着…感受』\nboth of which __(30)__ feelings of threat and distress——心率与出汗反应『标示着威胁\n与痛苦的感受』，signify 正是『作为…的标志/表征』的常用动词，与生理指标作为情绪指\n标的科学表述完全契合。\n【竞争词】O) surpass 义为『超过』，与情绪感受无法搭配\nsurpass feelings of threat 表『超越威胁感』，宾语与动词意义不匹配，逻辑荒谬。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·定语从句主语 both of which"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adversely", "B) amiably", "C) boost", "D) cognitive", "E) compassion", "F) connected", "G) correlation", "H) fascinated", "I) indignantly", "J) insulation", "K) lavish", "L) prone", "M) recurrent", "N) signify", "O) surpass"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) boost\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 can 后须接动词原形\n【依据】boost the immune system 是固定搭配\ncan __(31)__ a person's immune system. This, in turn, gives them a greater likelihood\nof recovering quickly from illness——『增强免疫系统，从而更易病愈』，boost the\nimmune system 是健康/医学语境中的固定表达，逻辑链清晰。\n【竞争词】N) signify 义为『表示/意味着』，与『对免疫系统起作用』语义错位\nsignify the immune system 表『标示免疫系统』，不通；且 N 在第 30 空有更精确的归宿。\nO) surpass 义为『超过』，宾语与免疫系统逻辑不符\nsurpass one's immune system 表『超越其免疫系统』，无意义。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 can 后须接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adversely", "B) amiably", "C) boost", "D) cognitive", "E) compassion", "F) connected", "G) correlation", "H) fascinated", "I) indignantly", "J) insulation", "K) lavish", "L) prone", "M) recurrent", "N) signify", "O) surpass"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) recurrent\n形容词\n【词性槽】with 后修饰 depression 的前置定语\n【依据】recurrent depression 是临床精确术语\nindividuals with __(32)__ depression benefit particularly from self-awareness-based\ncognitive therapy 说的是抑郁症患者中的某一亚群——『反复发作型抑郁』（recurrent\ndepression）正是医学/心理学专业术语，且与下段 meeting their negative thoughts\nand feelings 中『反复出现的负性念头』自洽。\n【竞争词】F) connected 义为『相连的』，与 depression 搭不上\n没有 connected depression 这种表述。\nH) fascinated 形容人感受，不能修饰疾病\nfascinated depression 不通。\nK) lavish 色彩与疾病不符\nlavish 表『奢华的』，不修饰病症。\nL) prone prone 不前置修饰名词\nprone 多作表语 be prone to；且需留给第 34 空 prone to depression。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·with 后修饰 depression"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adversely", "B) amiably", "C) boost", "D) cognitive", "E) compassion", "F) connected", "G) correlation", "H) fascinated", "I) indignantly", "J) insulation", "K) lavish", "L) prone", "M) recurrent", "N) signify", "O) surpass"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) cognitive\n形容词\n【词性槽】self-awareness-based 与 therapy 之间，须填形容词作前置定语\n【依据】cognitive therapy 是心理学术语固定搭配\nself-awareness-based __(33)__ therapy——『基于自我觉察的认知疗法』是临床心理学权\n威术语 cognitive therapy / CBT 的直接对应，与下句 learn to become more\nsympathetic to themselves 的认知重构思路完全吻合。\n【竞争词】F) connected 义为『相连的』，与 therapy 搭不上\n没有 connected therapy 这种说法；且 connected 留待第 28 空 feel connected 用。\nH) fascinated 形容人，不能修饰 therapy\nfascinated 一般表『（人）着迷的』，主语应是 someone is fascinated；放在 therapy 前不\n成立。\nK) lavish 义为『奢华的/过度的』，色彩不符\nlavish therapy 不是临床术语，也与『自我觉察』的内省语境不搭。\nL) prone prone 后常接 to，作表语，不作前置定语\n标准结构是 be prone to N；几乎不放在名词前作 prone therapy；且留待第 34 空 prone\nto depression。\nM) recurrent 义为『反复发作的』，与 therapy 逻辑不符\nrecurrent 修饰的是疾病/症状（recurrent depression），不修饰疗法本身；且留待第 32 空。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— 考生易被 self-awareness-based 这串复合形容词带跑而选\nconnected。self-awareness-based __(33)__ therapy 中，看到『自我觉察』易联想『联\n结』而选 connected；防坑：心理学『___ therapy』 类术语优先匹配 cognitive，且\nconnected 留作 28 空的 feel connected to 才合理。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·self-awareness-based"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adversely", "B) amiably", "C) boost", "D) cognitive", "E) compassion", "F) connected", "G) correlation", "H) fascinated", "I) indignantly", "J) insulation", "K) lavish", "L) prone", "M) recurrent", "N) signify", "O) surpass"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) prone\n形容词\n【词性槽】for people 后修饰 people 的后置定语，且后接 to depression\n【依据】be prone to depression 是固定搭配\nfor people __(34)__ to depression——『易患抑郁的人群』，prone to + N 表『易于/倾\n向于（不好的事）』，与抑郁这种负面色彩完美匹配。\n【竞争词】F) connected connected 搭 with/to 都不表示『易患』\nconnected to depression 只表示『与抑郁相关』，不表『易患』，语义弱且偏题。\nH) fascinated fascinated to depression 不成立\nfascinated 通常搭 by/with，不搭 to；且『对抑郁着迷』逻辑荒谬。\nK) lavish lavish 不搭 to，色彩也反\nlavish 没有 lavish to 的固定结构。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·for people 后修饰 peopl"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) adversely", "B) amiably", "C) boost", "D) cognitive", "E) compassion", "F) connected", "G) correlation", "H) fascinated", "I) indignantly", "J) insulation", "K) lavish", "L) prone", "M) recurrent", "N) signify", "O) surpass"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) compassion\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 with 后须接名词作宾语\n【依据】与主旨『善待自己』扣合，meet ... with compassion 是地道搭配\nmeeting their negative thoughts and feelings with __(35)__——『以慈悲心面对自己的\n负面念头与感受』，与全文主线 Thinking kind thoughts about yourself 完全一致；且呼\n应 They essentially learn to become more sympathetic to themselves。\n【竞争词】J) insulation 义为『绝缘』，与情感主题完全脱节\nwith insulation 表『用绝缘材料』，物理工程语境，放进心理学语境荒谬。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 with 后须接名词作宾语"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Climate change has wrought havoc on the lives of tens of thousands of people.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】成千上万人的生活被气候变化摧毁\n【定位】climate change has already destroyed tens of thousands of livelihoods, and the situation will\nworsen in the near future for millions of mostly poorer people\n气候变化已经摧毁了数以万计的生计，而对几百万主要是较贫困的人来说，情况在不久的将\n来还会更糟。\n【改写】Climate change has wrought havoc on the lives of tens of thousands of people ↔\nclimate change has already destroyed tens of thousands of livelihoods\nwrought havoc=destroyed，lives of people=livelihoods\n【辨邻】 H 本段虽然也属于气候危机话题，但全段Scientific evidence that we are living in an\nera of climate change, resource exhaustion and potential ecological disaster is\noverwhelming谈的是证据规模，没有出现具体的受害人数与生计受损的事实，因此可\n排除。\nScientific evidence that we are living in an era of climate change, resource exhaustion and potential\necological disaster is overwhelming", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "It took scientists a long time to realise that the function of forests goes far beyond providing humans with timber.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】科学家很久才认识到森林功能远不止木材\n【定位】It would take more than three centuries before scientists began to understand that timber\nproduction is not the only, and possibly not the most important, function of forests.\n科学家又用了三个多世纪才开始理解：木材生产不是森林唯一的、可能也不是最重要的\n功能。\n【改写】It took scientists a long time to realise ↔\nIt would take more than three centuries before scientists began to understand\na long time=more than three centuries（具体上位概括）\nthe function of forests goes far beyond providing humans with timber ↔\ntimber production is not the only, and possibly not the most important, function of forests\ngoes far beyond=not the only，把否定句改成肯定的超出\n【辨邻】 A 本段主线是The idea of preserving resources came out of a concern for the\nfuture，从来没说科学家意识到森林功能不止木材，故不能作为答案段。\nThe idea of preserving resources came out of a concern for the future: a fear of using up resources\nfaster than they could be replenished\nC 本段开篇即Recent years have seen a big change in our view of forests，讲的是新\n近的转变，而陈述句强调的是历史性的漫长过程，时间轴不同。\nRecent years have seen a big change in our view of forests", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "There is abundant evidence that we are now facing a possible ecological disaster.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】大量证据指向可能的生态灾难\n【定位】Scientific evidence that we are living in an era of climate change, resource exhaustion and\npotential ecological disaster is overwhelming.\n我们正生活在一个气候变化、资源枯竭和潜在生态灾难时代——这一科学证据已经多到压倒\n性的程度。\n【改写】There is abundant evidence that we are now facing a possible ecological disaster ↔\nScientific evidence that we are living in an era of climate change, resource exhaustion and\npotential ecological disaster is overwhelming\nabundant=overwhelming，possible=potential，facing=living in an era of\n【辨邻】 F 本段核心信息是the Paris Conference nevertheless marked a historic step toward\nthe recognition of the need for action on climate change——讲的是政治回应，\n不是科学证据规模。\nclimate change has already destroyed tens of thousands of livelihoods, and the situation will worsen\nin the near future for millions of mostly poorer people\nE 本段强调The search for an adequate response to climate change occupies\ncentre stage in international diplomacy，重点在国际外交回应，没有任何关于证据\n充足/压倒性的陈述。\nThe search for an adequate response to climate change occupies centre stage in\ninternational diplomacy", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Environmental science became academic disciplines only some sixty years ago.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】六十多年前环境学才成为学科\n【定位】It was in 1956 that the very first bachelor of science in environmental studies was awarded, at\nthe State University of New York College of Forestry at Syracuse.\n正是在1956年，纽约州立大学雪城林学院授予了第一个环境学理学学士学位。\n【改写】Environmental science became academic disciplines only some sixty years ago ↔\nIt was in 1956 that the very first bachelor of science in environmental studies was awarded, at\nthe State University of New York College of Forestry at Syracuse\n1956年=六十多年前（具体年份化为相对时长）\nEnvironmental science became academic disciplines ↔\nenvironmental science as new, integrated academic disciplines\nacademic disciplines原样复现", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Things cannot change overnight, but reducing the consumption of natural resources will help solve the ecological crisis.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】事物不能一夜改变但减少消耗有助生态\n【定位】The concept of slow hope suggests that we can't expect things to change overnight. If the\never-faster exhaustion of natural resources (in ecological terms) and the \"shrinking of the\npresent\" (in social terms) are urgent problems of humans, then cutting down on exhaustive\npractices and working towards a \"stretching of the present\" will be ways to move forward.\n慢希望的概念指出，我们不能指望事情一夜之间改变。如果自然资源越来越快地耗竭（生态\n层面）和当下的萎缩（社会层面）是人类的紧迫问题，那么减少耗竭性的实践、努力让当下\n变得绵长，就是前进的途径。\n【改写】Things cannot change overnight ↔ we can't expect things to change overnight\novernight逐字一致\nreducing the consumption of natural resources will help solve the ecological crisis ↔\ncutting down on exhaustive practices and working towards a \"stretching of the present\" will\nbe ways to move forward\ncutting down on exhaustive practices=reducing consumption\n【辨邻】 J 本段But if we believe that nothing can be changed, then we are giving up our\nopportunity to act讲的是不能放弃信念，并未陈述事物不能一夜改变这一时间维度的\n判断，故不能作为答案段。\nBut if we believe that nothing can be changed, then we are giving up our opportunity to act", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Human perception of forests has undergone a tremendous change in the past years.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】人类对森林的看法近年发生巨大变化\n【定位】Recent years have seen a big change in our view of forests.\n近些年来，我们看待森林的方式发生了巨大的变化。\n【改写】Human perception of forests has undergone a tremendous change in the past years ↔\nRecent years have seen a big change in our view of forests\ntremendous=big，perception=view，past years=Recent years\n【辨邻】 B 本段时间锚点是The late 19th and early 20th century saw an increasing\nrecognition that forests serve as habitats，与陈述句强调的近年发生的巨大变化在\n时间上明显错位。\nThe late 19th and early 20th century saw an increasing recognition that forests serve as habitats for\ncountless animal and plant species", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Recent history shows reduction of pollution, once seemingly impossible, can actually be accomplished.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】近代史显示曾以为不可能的减污也能做到\n【定位】The current crisis is not the first that humans have encountered, and a look at the struggles\nwith pollution in recent history reveals transformations that once seemed unimaginable.\n当前的危机并不是人类遇到的第一场危机，回望近代史上与污染的搏斗，可以发现一些曾经\n看似不可想象的转变是真的发生了的。\n【改写】Recent history shows reduction of pollution, once seemingly impossible, can actually be\naccomplished\n↔\na look at the struggles with pollution in recent history reveals transformations that once\nseemed unimaginable\nseemingly impossible=once seemed unimaginable，reveals=shows\n【辨邻】 H 本段问的是How do we motivate a public exhausted by never-ending scenarios\nof doom and disaster——重点在动员难度，没有讲污染治理曾被认为不可能、最后\n做到了这一事实。\nHow do we motivate a public exhausted by never-ending scenarios of doom and disaster, when the\nchallenges seem so huge and so impossible to solve?\nJ 本段的成功故事清单是the rise of urban gardening and of renaturalised\nriverscapes, of successful protests against polluted air and water, of the rise of\nregional markets and slow food, and the planting of trees around the globe\n——并未提及近代史上的污染治理本身曾被认为不可能这一具体事实。\nthe rise of urban gardening and of renaturalised riverscapes, of successful protests against polluted\nair and water", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "People began to consider preserving natural resources when they feared they would have nothing to use in the future.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】因担心未来无可用才开始保护资源\n【定位】The idea of preserving resources came out of a concern for the future: a fear of using up\nresources faster than they could be replenished.\n资源保护的观念，源自对未来的担忧——担心资源被用尽的速度快于它们被补充的速度。\n【改写】People began to consider preserving natural resources ↔\nThe idea of preserving resources came out of a concern for the future\nbegan to consider=came out of\nwhen they feared they would have nothing to use in the future ↔\na fear of using up resources faster than they could be replenished\nfeared having nothing to use=fear of using up resources", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "If we doubt our ability to reverse ecological deterioration, we are throwing away the chance to take action.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】若怀疑能扭转生态恶化就是放弃行动机会\n【定位】But if we believe that nothing can be changed, then we are giving up our opportunity to act.\n但如果我们相信什么都改变不了，那就等于在放弃自己行动的机会。\n【改写】If we doubt our ability to reverse ecological deterioration ↔\nif we believe that nothing can be changed\ndoubt our ability to reverse=believe nothing can be changed\nwe are throwing away the chance to take action ↔ we are giving up our opportunity to act\nthrowing away=giving up，chance=opportunity，take action=act\n【辨邻】 K 本段Hope can work as a wakeup call. It acknowledges setbacks. The concept of\nslow hope suggests that we can't expect things to change overnight——焦点在\n希望的时间维度（不能一夜改变），没有把怀疑能力等同于放弃行动机会这一条件结果\n命题。\nThe concept of slow hope suggests that we can't expect things to change overnight\nI 本段We need stories and histories of change and transformation——核心呼吁是\n讲述变化故事，没有提到对能力的怀疑会导致放弃行动机会。\nWe need stories and histories of change and transformation: ecological stories that make us confront\nthe fact that human power is potentially destructive", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "How to respond effectively to climate change has become the focus of international diplomacy.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】应对气候变化成为国际外交的焦点\n【定位】The search for an adequate response to climate change occupies centre stage in\ninternational diplomacy.\n为气候变化寻找适当的应对方案，占据着国际外交舞台的中心。\n【改写】How to respond effectively to climate change has become the focus of international diplomacy ↔\nThe search for an adequate response to climate change occupies centre stage in international\ndiplomacy\nthe focus=centre stage，respond effectively=adequate response\n【辨邻】 F 本段But the Paris Conference nevertheless marked a historic step toward the\nrecognition of the need for action on climate change——讲的是巴黎大会的历史\n意义，没有把应对气候变化总体定位为国际外交的焦点。\nthe Paris Agreement of the United Nations Climate Change Conference in December 2015\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 从中世纪到1548年威尼斯人估算木材只够用30年的故事切入：人类对森林长期是粗暴\n破坏，但保护资源的念头最早起于对未来用尽木材的担忧。\n信息指纹 1548年威尼斯30年木材忧虑\nB 讲科学家用了三百多年才意识到：森林不仅产木材，更是栖息地、防止水土流失、调\n节水分、过滤污染、产氧、提供休闲与保护历史遗迹。\n信息指纹 森林产木材以外的多重功能\nC 借2015年畅销书《The Hidden Life of Trees》介绍树木之间能通过根系与真菌组成的\nwood wide web互相预警、共享养分，使人类近年对森林的认识发生了深刻变化。\n信息指纹 2015年畅销书与wood wide web\nD 讲environment这个概念在1940年代才出现、1970年代普及，1956年第一个环境学本\n科学位授予，环境学因此成为新的学术学科。\n信息指纹 1956年第一个环境学本科学位\nE 环境观念与生态科学的兴起影响了新技术与政治，应对气候变化的方案占据了国际外\n交的中心位置。\n信息指纹 气候变化方案占国际外交中心\nF 评价2015年巴黎气候大会：批评者认为它不够，但它确是历史性一步；其中提到气候\n变化已经摧毁了成千上万人的生计、还会让数百万穷人继续受害。\n信息指纹 巴黎大会与tens of thousands of livelihoods\nG 用1952年伦敦大雾及随后《Clean Air Act》(1956)、2003年Congestion Charge\nZone、2019年Ultra Low Emission Zone为例，说明近代史上的污染治理曾被认为不可\n能、最后却做到了。\n信息指纹 1952伦敦大雾与污染治理史\nH 指出我们身处气候变化、资源枯竭与潜在生态灾难时代的科学证据已经压倒性地多；\n并追问：当挑战巨大时如何让已被末日叙事拖累的公众继续行动。\n信息指纹 overwhelming生态灾难证据\nI 呼吁要讲改变与转型的生态故事，让人类直面自身权力的破坏性，认识到物种存续依\n赖于土壤、水与栖息地的保护。\n信息指纹 呼唤改变与转型的生态故事\nJ 号召展示已发生的成功与生态修复案例：城市园艺、河流再自然化、抗污抗议、区域\n市场与慢食、全球植树等；并警告若我们相信一切无法改变，就是在放弃自己行动的\n机会。\n信息指纹 若信无法改变就是放弃行动机会\nK\n提出slow hope概念：救我们的力量不会来自单一神来之笔，事情不可能一夜改变，减\n少耗竭性的实践、追求当下的延展才是出路。\n信息指纹 slow hope与不能一夜改变", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "How do some musicians perceive using AI in creating music?", "options": ["A) It would help to produce more music idols.", "B) It would be detrimental to music production.", "C) It would hinder the understanding of authentic music.", "D) It would be the beginning of a new era in music creation."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干How do some musicians perceive using AI in creating music?问\"一部分音乐人如\n何看待\"，是对原文中明示观点句的客观转述，属于细节题而非推断题。\n【拆句】How do some musicians perceive using AI in creating music? 主干是 some musicians\nperceive X，X 是动名词短语 using AI in creating music。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Contrary to seeing AI as a tool, some musicians consider it to be hugely detrimental to the\nmusic scene.\n与把 AI 视作工具的看法相反，一些音乐人认为它对音乐界极为有害。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文首段提到 American idol star Taryn Southern 是引子人名，与音乐人对 AI 的看法\n无关；produce more music idols 拿这一无关词造句，属无中生有。\nB) ✓ 正确\nhugely detrimental to the music scene ↔ detrimental to music production\n预判得到\"对音乐创作有害\"，It would be detrimental to music production 是其逐词\n同义改写，最直接对应，故选 B。\nC) 偷换概念\n反对派担心的是 AI not necessarily what we want to hear\"产出质量\"和never\nemulate the work\"复刻原创\"，从未说过 AI 会妨碍\"听众理解\"真正的音乐；hinder the\nunderstanding of authentic music 把动作对象从\"产出\"偷换为\"理解\"。\nD) 与原文相反\nthe beginning of a new era\"新时代的开端\"是正向积极评价，而原文反对派恰恰说的\n是 the beginning of the end\"终结的开端\"——选项把 end 偷换成 new era，方向完全\n相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does Taryn Southern's story illustrate?", "options": ["A) AI technology is conducive to music composition.", "B) Musicians will be unable to create music without high tech.", "C) Musicians are often at their wits' end in their creative effort.", "D) AI technology is indispensable to creating melodies and chords."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （例证目的题）\n【判型】题干结构是\"具体人物故事 + illustrate\"，问例子的服务对象，是典型的例证目的题。\n【拆句】What does Taryn Southern's story illustrate? 主干 What does X illustrate，X 是 Taryn\nSouthern's story。结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】Taryn's story has a distinctly positive feel that highlights the advantages of using AI in music\nproduction. It can serve as a source of inspiration, and as an ideal jumping-off point should a\nmusician be hit with writer's block.\nTaryn 的故事带有明显的积极意味，凸显出在音乐创作中使用 AI 的种种好处。它可以作为\n灵感的源泉，并且在音乐人遭遇文思枯竭时，是一个理想的起点。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nhighlights the advantages of using AI in music production ↔ conducive to music composition\n预判得到\"AI 有助于音乐创作\"，AI technology is conducive to music composition\n精确对应，故选 A。\nB) 过度推断\n原文只说 AI 是\"协作工具\"\"灵感来源\"，从未说\"没有高科技就无法创作\"；unable to\ncreate music without high tech 是把\"有帮助\"夸大成\"不可或缺\"。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文只说\"如果\"音乐人遇到 writer's block，AI 是一个 jumping-off point；选项 often\nat their wits' end\"经常技穷\"把假设条件偷换为普遍现象，且偏离例证目的。\nD) 过度推断\n原文称 AI 是 source of inspiration 与 jumping-off point——是\"加分项\"，而\nindispensable to creating melodies and chords\"不可或缺\"把作用拔高到\"必需\"，与\n原文协作性定位不符。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Why are some musicians opposed to the use of AI in creating music?", "options": ["A) Music produced with AI technology lacks humanness.", "B) Music created with AI technology is easily emulated.", "C) It will depreciate humans' role in music composition.", "D) It will deplete young musicians, creative inspiration."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干以 Why 提问反对的原因，答案应是原文里反对派给出的明确理由句的同义改写。\n【拆句】Why are some musicians opposed to the use of AI in creating music? be opposed to\n是固定搭配=反对。主干清晰。\n【定位】Those who have produced their own music, or even fans of authentic, artistic music, will also\nargue that a computer could never emulate the work (and human touch) of a true musician.\n那些自己创作过音乐的人，甚至是真正的艺术音乐爱好者也会争辩说，电脑永远无法复刻一\n位真正音乐人的作品（以及那种人性温度）。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na computer could never emulate the work (and human touch) of a true musician ↔\nlacks humanness\n预判\"缺人性\"与 Music produced with AI technology lacks humanness 完全等价，\n故选 A。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文说\"电脑无法 emulate 真正音乐人\"——是 AI 模仿不来人；选项 easily\nemulated\"AI 音乐容易被模仿\"把模仿的主客关系颠倒，且 easily 与原文 never 直接\n相反。\nC) 答非所问\ndepreciate humans' role\"贬低人类在音乐创作中的角色\"虽然在情感上和反对派接近，\n但原文给出的反对理由是\"复刻不出人性\"而非\"会贬低人类角色\"——本题问的是 Why 反\n对，必须是原文实际给出的理由。\nD) 无中生有\n原文从未提到 AI 会\"耗尽年轻音乐人的创作灵感\"，deplete young musicians, creative\ninspiration 是凭空造话。注意 P2 提到 AI 是\"灵感来源\"——正好相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "Why do many people think music is too precious to impart to AI technology?", "options": ["A) It cannot be created without pains.", "B) It cannot be produced at random.", "C) It is part of human life.", "D) It is human specific."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干问\"为何认为音乐太珍贵不能交给AI\"，是对原文给出理由的客观转述。\n【拆句】Why do many people think music is too precious to impart to AI technology? 嵌套\nthink 宾语从句；too…to… 表示\"太…以致不能\"；impart to=交付给。理解关键是 too…\nto… 的否定意味。\n【定位】Since many people see music as such an inherently human expression, it is often considered\nas too precious to impart to technology.\n因为许多人把音乐视为人类固有的一种表达方式，所以它常被认为太珍贵了，不应交付给\n技术。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n原文用 painstakingly\"煞费苦心\"形容真正音乐人的创作，强调过程不易；cannot be\ncreated without pains\"没有痛苦就无法创作\"虽与文本有触点，但本题问的是\"为何太珍\n贵\"，答案应是\"音乐属于人类\"，而不是\"创作过程辛苦\"。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文说\"电脑生成 random 的音乐被视为亵渎\"，并非\"音乐不能随机产生\"；cannot be\nproduced at random 把原文的 random（贬义形容词修饰AI产品）偷换为\"产生方\n式\"的逻辑命题，且并非太珍贵的理由。\nC) 以偏概全\npart of human life\"是人类生活的一部分\"虽然符合原文\"陪伴人类多年\"的事实\n（integral part of the story of humans for ages），但本题问的是\"为何太珍贵\"，关\n键论点是\"人类专属\"而非\"陪伴时间长\"。Part of human life 比 human specific 弱：很\n多东西都是\"人类生活的一部分\"（如食物、衣物），未必只属人类——这恰恰落不到\"专\n属\"的关键力度。\nD) ✓ 正确\nan inherently human expression ↔ human specific\n预判\"音乐是人类专属\"与 It is human specific 完全等价，故选 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author think of the future of AI music?", "options": ["A) It will continue to arouse the interest of music investors.", "B) It has the prospect of becoming the norm in the future.", "C) It will be gradually accepted by old-school musicians.", "D) It may eventually lose its freshness and appeal."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度题）\n【判型】题干问作者怎么看，是对作者主观立场的探测，属态度/观点题。\n【拆句】What does the author think of the future of AI music? think of=对…怎么看；of 引出\n态度对象。主干平铺。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\ncontinue to arouse the interest of music investors 投资者的兴趣，在原文 with\nmore investors interested in the development 中只是作者用来支持\"AI 音乐必成常\n态\"的论据，本身不是作者对\"未来\"的核心判断；本题问 What does the author think\nof the future，答案要选最终判断而不是中间论据。\nB) ✓ 正确\nit's only a matter of time before AI-produced music is seen as the new norm ↔\nhas the prospect of becoming the norm in the future\n预判\"AI 音乐终将成为常态\"与 It has the prospect of becoming the norm in the\nfuture 同义，故选 B。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 there are always bound to be stubborn old-school musicians who refuse to\nuse tech 明确指出守旧派始终会拒绝技术；gradually accepted by old-school\nmusicians 反过来说他们会\"逐渐接受\"，与原文 refuse 直接相反。\nD) 无中生有\nmay eventually lose its freshness and appeal\"最终失去新鲜感和吸引力\"——原文从\n未提到 AI 音乐会失去吸引力，反而判断它会成为新常态。属凭空构造。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say about communicating science to the general public?", "options": ["A) It will help them to keep abreast of the latest scientific developments.", "B) It is a necessary means to improve their understanding of scientific issues.", "C) It will get them more involved in academic debates on environmental problems.", "D) It is an effective way to augment scientists' influence beyond the academic circle."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about communicating science to the general public? 问\n的是「作者对某话题说了什么」，要求回到原文找出作者关于这件事的明确表态，属于细节\n理解。\n【拆句】Q51 是简单的 What 引导特殊疑问句，主干是 the author say about 后接动名词短语\ncommunicating science to the general public，无从句、无否定、无陷阱。\n【定位】Communicating science beyond the academic bubble is necessary to augmenting public\nunderstanding of health and environmental issues and helping individuals make well-\ninformed personal decisions.\n把科学传播到学术圈外，是增进公众对健康与环境议题理解、帮助个人做出明智决策的必要\n途径。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nkeep abreast of the latest scientific developments 把「最新科学进展」塞进选项，\n而 P2 只说提升理解、辅助决策，从未提及 latest developments。\nB) ✓ 正确\nnecessary to augmenting public understanding of health and environmental issues ↔\na necessary means to improve their understanding of scientific issues\na necessary means to improve their understanding of scientific issues 同时命中预\n判的「必要性」与「提升理解」两条语义槽，无多余成分，是定位句的标准同义改写。\nC) 偷换概念\nget them more involved in academic debates on environmental problems 把原文\n「帮助个人做明智决策」偷换为「让公众参与学术辩论」，方向完全相反——原文恰恰\n是要把科学带到学术圈外，而非把公众拉进学术辩论。\nD) 张冠李戴\naugment scientists' influence beyond the academic circle 把「augment 的对象」\n从原文的 public understanding 偷换为 scientists' influence——augment 这个词留\n下了，受益人却换了，是典型「同词不同对象」陷阱。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author advise scientists to do to deal with topics outside of their specialty?", "options": ["A) Write or present on them from new angles.", "B) Utilize information from diverse sources.", "C) Turn to a specialist for professional help.", "D) Fact-check with colleagues in their field."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author advise scientists to do to deal with topics outside of their\nspecialty? 直接问「作者建议做什么」，是对原文具体建议句的事实复述。\n【拆句】Q52 主干 What does the author advise scientists to do，后接不定式短语 to deal with\ntopics outside of their specialty 表目的，整体直白。\n【定位】That is not to suggest that they only write or present on their own research, but rather, that\nthey consult with an expert if the topic is outside of their discipline.\n这并不是说他们只能写或讲自己的研究，而是说，如果话题超出本人学科范围，他们应当请\n教该领域专家。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nWrite or present on them from new angles 凭空加入 new angles 这一限定，原文\nthey only write or present on their own research 是作者要排除的误读，且无任何\n「新角度」之意。\nB) 偷换概念\nUtilize information from diverse sources 把作者明确指出的「咨询专家 + 事实核查」\n泛化为「使用多元来源信息」，方向被悄悄稀释——作者强调的是「找对人」，不是\n「找够多源头」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nconsult with an expert if the topic is outside of their discipline ↔\nTurn to a specialist for professional help\nTurn to a specialist for professional help 把预判的「请教 + 该领域专家」两要素逐一\n对上，且 specialist 与 specialty 同根，符合作者「领域内行家」的限定。\nD) 张冠李戴\nFact-check with colleagues in their field 保留 Fact-check 这一动词（直接借自原文\nFact-checking with a scientist who works in the specialty），但把对象偷换为\ncolleagues in their field——原文恰恰强调因为话题在 their field 之外，才要找该话题\n所在领域（specialty）的专家。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author say we can learn from social media?", "options": ["A) A solid academic foundation is essential to understanding baffling scientific principles.", "B) Modern technology has facilitated communication between scientists and the public.", "C) Scientific articles have gained increasing popularity among the general public.", "D) The public's understanding of science is much better than some have claimed."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say we can learn from social media? 问的是「从某事例中能得\n出什么结论」，本质仍是回到原文找作者就 social media 给出的论断句，属于细节而非\n推断。\n【拆句】Q53 主干 What… we can learn from social media，宾语从句简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】If anything, social media has shown that the public craves knowledge based on a solid\nscientific foundation. Even the public discourse that follows most scientific articles shows\nthat online readers can understand even the most baffling of scientific principles.\n如果说有什么的话，社交媒体反而表明，公众渴望以扎实科学基础为支撑的知识。甚至大多\n数科学文章后面的公众讨论都显示，在线读者能够理解最难懂的科学原理。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nA solid academic foundation is essential to understanding baffling scientific\nprinciples 把原文「公众渴望基于科学的知识」误读为「必须具备学术根基才能理解科\n学」——原文是「公众主动 craves 知识」，不是「没有 foundation 就不行」。\nB) 偷换概念\nModern technology has facilitated communication between scientists and the\npublic 把 social media 上位概括为 modern technology，又把「公众理解力」偷换为\n「双向沟通」，原文从未谈及 facilitated communication。\nC) 以偏概全\nScientific articles have gained increasing popularity among the general public 把\n原文用作佐证的 the public discourse that follows most scientific articles 偷换为\n「科学文章越来越受欢迎」——原文谈的是文章后面的「公众讨论显示理解力」，不是\n文章本身受欢迎程度。\nD) ✓ 正确\nonline readers can understand even the most baffling of scientific principles ↔\nThe public's understanding of science is much better than some have claimed\nSome have argued that the public is not educated enough to understand scientific information…\nbut this is absurd\n↔ much better than some have claimed\nThe public's understanding of science is much better than some have claimed 把\n作者用 social media 反驳「公众不够聪明」的结论一一对应——选项里 some have\nclaimed 与原文 Some have argued 同源指代，结论同向。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the example of the biologist who shared an intricate analogy show?", "options": ["A) It is helpful to use illustrations in explaining scientific phenomena.", "B) It is imperative to have appropriate titles to explain scientific issues.", "C) A learned scholar is not necessarily a qualified science communicator.", "D) A nonscientific audience cannot duly understand principles of science."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （例证目的题）\n【判型】What does the example of the biologist who shared an intricate analogy show? 直接\n问「这个例子说明了什么」，是典型例证目的题。\n【拆句】Q54 主干 What does the example show，example 后接 of 短语 + who 引导的定语从句\n修饰 biologist，从句虽长但仅作识别身份用。\n【定位】It is equally imperative to emphasize that being an expert on a topic does not automatically\nmake a scholar qualified to communicate it to a nonscientific audience.\n同样必须强调的是，成为某话题的专家并不自动使学者具备向非科学受众传播该话题的\n资格。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt is helpful to use illustrations in explaining scientific phenomena 把「复杂类比」\n当成正面建议，与作者用此例所要表达的「这种类比反而不清晰」相反——作者明确说\nnot a clear example for public consumption。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt is imperative to have appropriate titles to explain scientific issues 把原文\nappropriate credentials 偷换为 appropriate titles，且作者强调的恰恰是「光有\ncredentials 不够」，本选项反而说「必须有合适头衔」，方向与作者完全相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nbeing an expert on a topic does not automatically make a scholar qualified to communicate it to\na nonscientific audience\n↔ A learned scholar is not necessarily a qualified science communicator\nEven though they have appropriate credentials, they often do very little in the way of explaining.\n↔ A learned scholar is not necessarily a qualified science communicator\nA learned scholar is not necessarily a qualified science communicator 把作者「专\n家≠科普者」的论点压缩成一句格言式陈述，与论点句一对一。\nD) 张冠李戴\nA nonscientific audience cannot duly understand principles of science 把矛头指向\n受众理解力不足，与本段乃至 P4 的论调（公众有理解力，问题在科学家不会讲）相\n反；本题考点是「专家≠科普者」，与受众能力无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2022-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author suggest scientists do to close the gap between science and society?", "options": ["A) Explain complex concepts scientifically.", "B) Make appropriate use of scientific terms.", "C) Take courses in public speaking.", "D) Develop communication skills."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest scientists do to close the gap between science and\nsociety? 仍是问「作者建议做什么」，是对原文末段建议句的事实复述。\n【拆句】Q55 主干 What does the author suggest scientists do，后接不定式 to close the gap\nbetween science and society 表目的，suggest 后接省略 that 的从句结构 scientists\n(should) do，是六级常考的虚拟语气省略，需要识别。\n【定位】A scientist should take communication courses that can teach a person how to identify and\neliminate jargon and how to develop effective analogies to explain complex concepts.\n科学家应当修习传播课程，这些课程能教人如何识别并消除行话、如何构造有效的类比以解\n释复杂概念。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nExplain complex concepts scientifically 把建议对象偷换为「科学地解释复杂概念」\n——原文要做的恰好相反，是用 effective analogies 把复杂概念变得通俗，而不是「更\n科学地」讲。\nB) 与原文相反\nMake appropriate use of scientific terms 与作者建议背道而驰——原文明确说\nidentify and eliminate jargon，选项却让科学家「合理使用术语」，方向反置。\nC) 以偏概全\nTake courses in public speaking 把原文 communication courses 偷换为 public\nspeaking——「沟通课」覆盖书面/口头/类比/受众分析等多个维度，而 public\nspeaking 只是其中一种，且方向更窄。\nD) ✓ 正确\ntake communication courses that can teach a person how to identify and eliminate jargon and\nhow to develop effective analogies\n↔ Develop communication skills\nScience communication is a science in and of itself. It requires rigorous training and instruction.\n↔ Develop communication skills\nDevelop communication skills 用最高一级的概括把原文末段全部建议吃下，且与段首\n主张 Science communication is a science in and of itself 完全同向，是稳妥的正确\n答案。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2022-06-1"});
